# zenavif 4K ENCODE peak-RSS measurement — 2026-08-13 (macOS arm64)
#
# Purpose: answer "how much RAM does a system need to encode a 4K AVIF?" at
# real 4K, on both usable AV1 encode backends. The prior calibration
# (benchmarks/zenavif_encode_mem_2026-06-23.tsv) topped out at 2048^2 = 4.19 MP
# and covered only the zenravif backend at its default 4:4:4; 8.3 MP and the
# SvtRs backend are measured here rather than extrapolated (CLAUDE.md
# never-extrapolate-memory).
#
# Commits under test:
#   zenavif      a311b42 (0.1.7)
#   ravif/zenravif cb06487   zenav1-svt 841c583bf
#   rustc 1.97.1 (8bab26f4f 2026-07-14), release profile
# Host: macOS 26.5.2, Apple M4 Pro (12 cores / 8 performance), 24 GiB RAM.
#   NOTE: the 2026-06-23 calibration ran on WSL2 x86-64 / glibc. This is a
#   DIFFERENT platform (arm64, macOS libmalloc), so absolute peaks are not
#   directly comparable to that file — they are a second data point, not a
#   re-fit of the shipped constants. No constant in src/heuristics.rs was
#   changed on the strength of this run.
#
# Harness: examples/mem_probe_encode.rs, one encode per process.
#   This run added the `--backend`, `--subsampling` and `--threads` axis flags
#   to that example (previously it hard-pinned zenravif / default 4:4:4 /
#   threads=1), so all three arms go through one harness.
#
# MEASUREMENT: peak RSS is `/usr/bin/time -l` "maximum resident set size",
#   which on macOS is reported in BYTES. The probe's own pre_rss_kb/vmhwm_kb
#   columns read /proc/self/status and are therefore 0 here — use
#   peak_rss_bytes. That number is the WHOLE-PROCESS high-water mark:
#   binary floor + the 24.9 MB raw input buffer + the packed Vec<Rgb<u8>>
#   copy + the encoder working set. It is the "will it fit in the box"
#   figure, NOT the marginal working set the heuristics constants model.
#
# Content: real corpus images MOSAICKED at native scale (no upscaling — an
#   upscale smooths high-frequency detail and would under-state coefficient
#   density, hence memory). photo = 6 CLIC2025 training photos; screen =
#   gb82-sc/imac_g3 + imageflow whitespace-issue + a wikimedia screenshot.
#
# Grid (160 cells, all measured, 0 failures):
#   canvas:      3840x2176 (8.36 MP) for the 3-arm comparison — SvtRs requires
#                both dimensions to be a multiple of 64 and 2160 is not;
#                3840x2160 (8.29 MP, true UHD) for the zenravif arms.
#   arms:        zenravif 4:4:4 (shipped default), zenravif 4:2:0, svtrs 4:2:0
#   speed:       6, 10   quality: 50, 85   threads: 1, 8
#   content:     photo, screen            reps: 2
#
# RESULTS — max peak RSS over content x quality x reps, 3840x2176:
#   arm                 speed  thr    max MB   med MB   max wall s
#   zenravif 4:2:0        6     1      173      166       16.9
#   zenravif 4:2:0        6     8      189      175        4.6
#   zenravif 4:2:0       10     1      186      183        1.5
#   zenravif 4:2:0       10     8      212      210        0.4
#   zenravif 4:4:4        6     1      207      200       22.2
#   zenravif 4:4:4        6     8      222      205        5.6
#   zenravif 4:4:4       10     1      231      225        2.3
#   zenravif 4:4:4       10     8      270      267        0.7
#   svtrs    4:2:0        6     1      434      426        0.7
#   svtrs    4:2:0       10     8      429      421        0.3
#   True UHD 3840x2160 matches within noise: zenravif 4:4:4 max 274 MB,
#   4:2:0 max 218 MB.
#
# Findings:
#   * Whole-arm envelope at 4K: zenravif <= 274 MB, SvtRs <= 434 MB.
#   * SvtRs costs ~2x the memory of zenravif at equal 4:2:0 subsampling
#     (434 vs 218 MB) and is ~20-50x faster in wall time (0.3-0.7 s vs
#     1.5-22 s). Its peak is nearly FLAT across speed, quality and threads —
#     the pipeline allocates its full frame working set up front.
#   * Chroma subsampling is a real memory axis: 4:4:4 costs ~25-30% more
#     than 4:2:0 (274 vs 218 MB at UHD).
#   * threads 1->8 adds ~15-20% to zenravif's peak (AV1 tile/row contexts)
#     and ~0% to SvtRs's.
#   * Speed 10 holds MORE memory than speed 6 (231 vs 207 MB, 4:4:4 t1),
#     consistent with the 2026-06-23 finding that the fast modes buffer more
#     while low-speed RDO searches depth-first.
#   * Content spread (photo vs screen) is <= 3% at 4K — much flatter than the
#     1.26x seen at <= 2048^2.
#
# MODEL CHECK vs src/heuristics.rs estimate_encode (RGB8, 3840x2160, speed 10):
#   model min/typ/max = 263.4 / 321.9 / 556.0 MB at 3840x2160
#                       265.3 / 324.2 / 560.1 MB at 3840x2176
#   zenravif 4:4:4 measured max 274.3 MB -> TYP over-predicts 1.17x  (safe)
#   zenravif 4:2:0 measured max 217.7 MB -> TYP over-predicts 1.48x  (safe)
#   svtrs    4:2:0 measured max 433.9 MB -> TYP UNDER-predicts (measured is
#     1.34x TYP); MAX still covers it with a 1.29x margin.
#   => estimate_encode is calibrated for zenravif only. It is NOT
#      backend-aware, and its TYP tier under-reports SvtRs. Callers sizing a
#      cap for an SvtRs encode must use peak_memory_bytes_max, not the
#      typical. Making the estimate backend-aware is untracked work as of
#      this file.
#
# Columns: codec backend subsampling content w h pixels mode effort quality
#          threads rep out_bytes encode_ms peak_rss_bytes wall_s
#   encode_ms is empty for AVIF (the probe reports it for JXL only); use
#   wall_s, which includes process start + the 24.9 MB file read.
